7:- Script lifecycle (US-056, `engine/script/src/script_runner.cpp`): each attached instance runs in its own Lua environment, a table whose reads fall through to `_G`. Callbacks are read with `lua_rawget`, so a shared global of the same name never runs. Teardown releases the environment reference and every event subscription the instance owns (`EventBus::set_owner`/`release_owner`).
8-- A callback error marks only its instance `Faulted`; `destroy` does not run for a faulted instance. Reload compiles the new chunk before touching the old code, so a syntax error keeps the running instance.
9-
10-- Lua C API bindings (US-055, `engine/script/src/script_bindings.cpp`): a binding raises with `luaL_error`/`luaL_argerror`, which longjmps past C++ frames. Such a frame must hold no `std::string` or other owning object when it can raise, so build owned values only after the checks. Entities cross as an `ecs::EntityHandle` in userdata, never a pointer, and each use checks `registry.world().alive()`.
--
